Understanding the Mathematics of Subnetting:
Every IPv4 address contains 32 bits divided into a Network Portion and a Host Portion.
Total IPv4 Bits = 32 = Network Bits + Host Bits

### 01. Common calculation and CONFUSION for calculating the SUBNET mask and HOST bits.
Step-by-Step Breakdown

In binary (base-2), each position represents a power of 2, starting from the right (2^0) to the left (2^7)

----------------------------------------------------------------------------
| Bit Position (from left) | 1st | 2nd | 3rd | 4th | 5th | 6th | 7th | 8th |
| ------------------------ | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Binary Value             |  1  |  1  |  1  |  1  |  1  |  1  |  1  |  1  |
| Decimal Place Value      | 128 | 64  | 32  | 16  | 8   | 4   | 2   |  1  |
|                          | 2^7 | 2^6 | 2^5 | 2^4 | 2^3 | 2^2 | 2^1 | 2^0 |
----------------------------------------------------------------------------

To convert to decimal, add up all the place values where the bit is 1
The decimal value of  11111111  is 255.
128 + 64 + 32 + 16 + 8 + 4 + 2 + 1 = 255


